## 🧠 Architecture Governance & Evolution

This project is governed by Archon. **Do not manually create random files or break the structure.** 
To add new domains, entities, or fields, use the **Archon AI Bridge**.

### Setup the AI Bridge

In order for your IDE AI to understand how to automatically modify this project, you need to configure the **Model Context Protocol (MCP)**.

1. **Install the Client** (Done automatically by `ARCHON.sh`):
   ```bash
   npm install -g archonspecs
   ```

2. **Configure your IDE**:

   Copy the following JSON literal:
   ```json
   {
     "mcpServers": {
       "archon-governance": {
         "command": "npx",
         "args": ["-y", "archonspecs", "https://archonspecs.dev/mcp/sse?apiKey=<YOUR_API_KEY>"]
       }
     }
   }
   ```
   *Note: Replace `<YOUR_API_KEY>` with your actual Archon platform token. If you have your own enterprise Archon server, replace the base URL with your remote endpoint.*

    *   **Cursor**: Go to `Cursor Settings > Features > MCP` and click `+ Add New MCP Server`.
        *   Name: `archon`
        *   Type: `command`
        *   Command: `npx -y archonspecs https://archonspecs.dev/mcp/sse?apiKey=<YOUR_API_KEY>`
    *   **Claude Desktop**: Open `~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json` and paste the JSON block inside the root `{}`.
    *   **Antigravity**: Open `~/.gemini/antigravity/mcp_config.json` and paste the JSON block inside the root `{}`.

3. **Talk to the AI**:
   Reload your IDE to apply the MCP settings. Open your IDE chat in this project repository and say:
   > *"Add a Notifications domain with a Webhook entity."*
   
   The AI will automatically use the MCP tools to update the remote spec and synchronize your local project, **preserving every method you marked with `@ArchonManual()`** (see "Evolving This Project" below).

## 🔄 Evolving This Project

Archon is a **hybrid developer**: it owns the architecture (entities, DTOs, CRUD, Swagger, SQL schema) and you own the specialized business logic. When the spec evolves and the project is re-materialized, the engine performs a **structural merge** — it never blindly overwrites your file.

### What happens on each regeneration

| You add… | Archon does (additively — nothing is deleted) |
| :--- | :--- |
| A **new entity** | Creates its entity/DTO/service/controller and adds a new table to `src/db/schema.sql` (`CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`) |
| A **new field** | Adds the `@Column` to the existing entity + `@ApiProperty` to the DTO, and emits `ALTER TABLE … ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS` in `schema.sql` |
| A **new operation** | Adds a protected `@ArchonManual()` stub to the service + a typed route to the controller |
| **Nothing** (re-run) | Standard CRUD methods are refreshed from the template; your protected code is left untouched |

The generated `schema.sql` is fully **idempotent** — running it repeatedly only adds what's missing, so the database stays in sync as features are added.

### 🛡️ Protecting your business logic — `@ArchonManual()`

This is the contract that keeps your code safe across regenerations:

```typescript
import { ArchonManual } from '../../shared/decorators/archon-manual.decorator';

@ArchonManual()
async registerAttendee(dto: any) {
  // 🖐️ Your logic. Archon will NEVER overwrite this method again.
  const profile = this.repo.create({ ...dto, status: 'REGISTERED' });
  return this.repo.save(profile);
}
```

Rules the merge engine follows:
- A **method** marked `@ArchonManual()` is **skipped entirely** on regeneration — your implementation is preserved verbatim.
- All generated **custom operations already carry `@ArchonManual()`**, so the moment you implement one it is locked.
- Want to customize a standard CRUD method (`create`, `findOne`, …)? Just add `@ArchonManual()` to it and it becomes yours.
- To freeze an **entire file**, put `@ArchonManual()` on the class — the whole file is then ignored by the engine.
- Merges are **additive**: new methods, properties and imports are added; existing protected code and your manual additions are never removed.

> ⚠️ Regenerating from a **stale spec** still won't touch `@ArchonManual` code, but it will refresh boilerplate. Keep the spec the source of truth — make structural changes there, business logic here.

